WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
Welcome to your inpatient psychiatry clerkship.

2
00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:06.240
Imagine stepping through those locked double doors right now.

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.280
I'll be your guide through this explainer today,

4
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.780
essentially acting as your virtual chief resident.

5
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.060
We're going to build a clear, structured foundation for your rotation.

6
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.420
And right off the bat, let's just get this out of the way.

7
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.300
Everything we cover here relies strictly on synthetic educational examples.

8
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.260
There is absolutely zero protected health information used in this explainer.

9
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.640
Keeping real patient details strictly within the clinical system

10
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.880
is a crucial discipline and honestly you'll be practicing that from today onward.

11
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.320
You know, it is completely normal to feel a little bit of anxiety

12
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.640
as you step onto the inpatient unit for the very first time.

13
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.880
But I want you to remember this.

14
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.120
Your job is not to know everything on day one.

15
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.920
Seriously, you aren't expected to instantly arrive at perfect diagnostic clarity.

16
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.040
Instead, your job is to be curious, prepared, and safe.

17
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.400
This rotation, it's really all about learning psychiatry as a process

18
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.720
of supervised clinical reasoning.

19
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.000
So here's our agenda for this explainer.

20
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.560
One, welcome to inpatient.

21
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.600
Two, your daily unit workflow.

22
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.320
Three, the clinical encounter.

23
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.600
Four, presenting your clinical reasoning.

24
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.400
Five, the single safety rule.

25
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.680
And six, day one essentials.

26
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.240
Let's jump right in.

27
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.640
Section one, welcome to inpatient psychiatry clerkship.

28
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.600
The inpatient unit is this incredibly dynamic environment

29
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.760
where acute psychiatric symptoms, complex medical risks,

30
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.960
and severe family stressors all kind of collide.

31
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.000
To navigate this successfully, you absolutely must understand your scope of practice.

32
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.440
Let's look at what you can do.

33
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.240
You are highly encouraged to interview patients, gather collateral information

34
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.600
with permission, of course, draft your clinical notes,

35
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.520
and present your formulation.

36
00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.000
But you also have strict boundaries.

37
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.360
You cannot make independent safety or discharge decisions.

38
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.520
You can't ever promise a patient they're going home.

39
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.160
You can't alter medications, grant unit privileges,

40
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.760
or contact a patient's family without explicit team approval.

41
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.040
Section two, your daily unit workflow team.

42
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.760
based care. Now knowing exactly where you need to be, hour by hour, really takes the

43
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.640
guesswork out of your day. And your day here revolves entirely around team-based care.

44
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.120
Before rounds, you're doing chart reviews, so checking vitals, sleep, and those overnight nursing

45
00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.480
notes. During rounds, you need to actively listen for safety risks and any barriers to discharge.

46
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.560
After rounds, that's when you'll actually see your assigned patients and gather that collateral

47
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.280
info. Mid-day is usually blocked off for teaching and consults. Then in the afternoon,

48
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.520
you present your clinical updates and revise your notes. Finally, at the end of the day,

49
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.520
you've got to clarify any handoff risks that might trigger an escalation overnight.

50
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.480
It's a full day, for sure. Section 3, the clinical encounter interview in MSC.

51
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.120
So it can be really tempting to treat a psychiatric interview like a rigid checklist,

52
00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.120
but honestly, it shouldn't be. Try to think of the interview as a flexible circle.

53
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.840
The patient's story lives right in the center. You flow outward to gather specific domains,

54
00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.200
things like safety, medical history, substance use, trauma,

55
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.120
but you always always return to the middle to flow naturally with their narrative.

56
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.840
It's a coverage map. You know, not a script you just read off of.

57
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.240
Now, when you document the observable mental status exam or MSC,

58
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.160
the absolute golden rule is to use objective observable language.

59
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.240
When describing a patient's behavior, please avoid vague or stigmatizing labels.

60
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.120
So instead of writing something like manipulative or non-compliant,

61
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.680
describe the exact behavior and the context. You can note that they were guarded but cooperative,

62
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.720
or literally describe exactly what the patient did in the room.

63
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.400
Your documentation has to show what you observed, not just a judgment you made.

64
00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.680
Section 4, presenting your clinical reasoning, formulation, and plan.

65
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.480
Once you've completed your clinical encounter, you need to synthesize all that information for

66
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.080
your attending. Now, students often stop at a differential diagnosis, which basically just asks,

67
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.440
what could this be? But an inpatient psychiatry, that's just not enough. You need a formulation.

68
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:59.040
A formulation goes way deeper. It asks why this patient, why right now, and what does this

69
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.000
actually mean for it?

70
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.000
treatment plan. You're going to build this reasoning into a

71
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.920
structured four-step formulation. Step one is body and brain. This

72
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.160
is paramount because any new or acutely changed psychiatric

73
00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.720
presentation is a medical workup until proven otherwise. So

74
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.960
consider sleep, substances, and medical mimics first. Step two

75
00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.600
is identifying the primary psychiatric syndrome. Step three

76
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.260
explores relationship and environment, things like family

77
00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.840
trauma or housing status. And finally, step four is recovery

78
00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.360
and role, which is understanding the patient's identity,

79
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.080
function, and what their life looks like post discharge. When it

80
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.080
comes time to present this formulation orally, stick to this

81
00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.520
rubric. A strong student presentation is above all, concise. You

82
00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.960
want to lead with the core clinical problem and establish a

83
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.160
clear timeline. Make sure to use that observable MSE language

84
00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.040
we just talked about, always include any medical and substance

85
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.440
contributors. And this is a big one, clearly separate chronic

86
00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.800
risk factors from acute, modifiable risk factors. Try not to

87
00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.680
over-present distant historical details. Keep your focus on

88
00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.360
what is actionable today. Section five, the single safety rule

89
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.400
escalate immediately. Okay, we have arrived at the absolute

90
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:13.840
most critical part of this explainer. It's a rule you must

91
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.280
commit to memory right now. If immediate safety is concerning,

92
00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:21.200
tell the resident or attending now, do not wait for rounds to

93
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.400
bring it up. And absolutely under no circumstances, do you

94
00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:28.160
carry that concern by yourself, escalate it immediately. This is

95
00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.840
literally the one non-negotiable rule of your clerkship. So

96
00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:37.000
what exactly constitutes an immediate safety concern? Well, here

97
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.120
are your immediate escalation triggers. If you observe current

98
00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.160
suicide or violence risk or escalating severe agitation, if you

99
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.480
see signs of withdrawal from alcohol or sedatives, or if you

100
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.920
notice severe rigidity, which could be catatonia, capacity and

101
00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.920
certainty, questions about involuntary care or any kind of

102
00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.560
medical instability. If any of these are present, you escalate. No

103
00:05:58.560 --> 00:05:59.480
questions asked.

104
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.980
I want to pull out one of those triggers specifically, delirium.

105
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.780
Delirium is an acute fluctuating disturbance

106
00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.460
of attention and awareness.

107
00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.860
You absolutely must view delirium as a medical emergency

108
00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:13.380
until proven otherwise.

109
00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.380
It's a massive psychiatric mimic,

110
00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.460
often driven by an underlying infection, hypoxia,

111
00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.300
or a heavy medication burden.

112
00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.060
If you see new confusion or inattention,

113
00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.820
especially in an older or medically ill patient,

114
00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:28.620
do not wait, escalate immediately.

115
00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.620
Section six, day one essentials, operational details.

116
00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.160
Finally, hear the operational details

117
00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.200
you need to secure the very moment you arrive on the unit.

118
00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.240
Because every single hospital system operates a bit differently,

119
00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.880
this is a gap you must verify locally today.

120
00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.200
Confirm your daily schedule

121
00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.680
and get the direct contact phone numbers

122
00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.240
for your resident and attending.

123
00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.920
Clarify the EMR rules, specifically what type of notes

124
00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.400
you're actually allowed to draft,

125
00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.680
and ensure you learn your local emergency procedures

126
00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.240
and the basics of involuntary hold forms.

127
00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.800
So, as you prepare to step onto the unit,

128
00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.440
I want you to ask yourself,

129
00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.680
how will you turn your observations

130
00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.440
into actionable clinical reasoning today?

131
00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.200
How will you use that flexible circle of an interview

132
00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:11.960
and those structured questions

133
00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.480
to build a compelling formulation

134
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:16.040
that actually drives patient care?

135
00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.320
Keep these three final points in mind

136
00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:19.920
as you begin your rotation.

137
00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.960
Number one, be curious.

138
00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.600
Don't rush to diagnostic labels

139
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.920
before understanding the patient's full context.

140
00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.320
Number two, be structured in your reasoning

141
00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.640
and your presentations.

142
00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:33.680
And number three, escalate safety concerns early and often.

143
00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.960
Psychiatry is a fascinating, profoundly impactful field

144
00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:37.760
of medicine.

145
00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.240
If you look closely, every patient's presentation

146
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.640
is telling a complex story of their brain,

147
00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.520
their body, and their environment.

148
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.280
The question is, are you paying close enough attention

149
